# Media Processing Conventions

## Image Processing (sharp)

- sharp handles all image formats: JPEG, PNG, WebP, TIFF, HEIC
- Auto-rotate based on EXIF orientation before any other operation
- Resize preserving aspect ratio (`sharp.resize({ fit: 'inside' })`)
- Sharpen small thumbnails (≤300px)
- JPEG quality: 100% for ≤300px thumbnails, 90% for larger
- For ONNX preprocessing: convert to raw RGB pixel buffer via `sharp.raw().toBuffer()`
- **Dimensions gotcha:** `metadata.width`/`height` return the raw pixel layout, ignoring EXIF orientation. Use `metadata.autoOrient.width`/`height` for post-rotation (display) dimensions. `getDimensions()` in `src/utils/image.ts` handles this.

## Thumbnails

- 5 resolution tiers: 1920x1080, 1280x720, 640x480, 300x300, 150x100
- Stored as individual JPEG files in `.thumbnails/` subdirectory
- Naming: `{name}_{width}.jpg`
- Skip tiers larger than the source image
- Atomic writes: temp file + rename

## Video Processing (FFmpeg)

- FFmpeg/ffprobe called via `child_process.execFile` (not `exec` — avoids shell injection)
- Frame extraction at 4-second mark for video thumbnails
- MOV → MP4: stream copy (fast). Other formats → MP4: libx264 + yuv420p + faststart
- Metadata via `ffprobe -v quiet -print_format json -show_format -show_streams`

## Face Detection (onnxruntime-node)

- YuNet ONNX model for detection, SFace for recognition
- Resize input to 600px max width for detection performance
- Scale coordinates back to original dimensions after detection
- Filter out images with >20 detections (false positive heuristic)
- Generate 300px max face thumbnail crops stored as blob in DB
- Cosine similarity for face comparison (threshold: 0.363)
- Transitive matching via BFS up to 10 levels deep

## Perceptual Hashing

- Resize to 32x32 grayscale → DCT → extract top-left 8x8 low-frequency coefficients → binary hash from median comparison
- Hamming distance via XOR + popcount
- Two-phase matching: in-memory cache for recent items, then batched DB scan

## Concurrency

- `p-limit` controls concurrent file processing (default: CPU core count)
- sharp and onnxruntime manage their own native thread pools
- Never use unbounded `Promise.all` on large file lists
